      en: "Magbele Historical Port, located on the Sierra Leone River 40 km east of Freetown, was a key slave trading post in the 18th and 19th centuries. Enslaved Africans were held in barracoons before being shipped across the Atlantic. After the abolition of the slave trade in 1807, the port transitioned to legitimate commerce, including palm oil and timber. Ruins of stone wharves and warehouses remain, along with a slave cemetery. Archaeological finds include trade beads, clay pipes, and European ceramics. The site is now a protected heritage area, visited as a place of remembrance. Local communities engage in storytelling tours. The port's history is documented in the Sierra Leone National Museum.",
